<section id="pocket" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">When a pocket device fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You fly more than one aircraft.</strong> A club aircraft, a rental, a friend&rsquo;s. Your visibility goes with you. A <%= link_to_page "skyblip-tango", label: "Tango" %> in the flight bag gives you the same radar in every cockpit.</li>
    <li><strong>A club equips its fleet.</strong> The price of one installed unit buys a Go for every aircraft in the air on a busy day. <%= link_to_page "blog/club-fleet", label: "How a club shares them" %>.</li>
    <li><strong>The cockpit has no room and no power.</strong> A microlight, a vintage aircraft or a homebuilt with a full panel. Go fits in a hand and runs a day on its battery.</li>
    <li><strong>You want no paperwork.</strong> A portable device needs no standard change and no engineer. You switch it on.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The cost is discipline: someone has to charge it and switch it on.</p>
</section>

<section id="installed" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">When an installed unit fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One aircraft, many pilots.</strong> It starts with the master switch, so it transmits on every flight.</li>
    <li><strong>You want the traffic on the panel.</strong> An installed unit can feed an EFIS through a serial link.</li>
    <li><strong>You want external antennas.</strong> Placed outside the cabin, they hear in every direction.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In an EASA type-certified aircraft, CS-STAN standard change CS-SC057a lets an engineer fit it without a design approval, at 300&nbsp;g or less, with a supplement to the flight manual and a release to service the pilot-owner cannot sign. <%= link_to_page "blog/always-on-board", label: "What CS-SC057a asks" %>. skyBlip has no installed device today. <%= link_to_page "roadmap", label: "skyBlip Panel", anchor: "on-board" %> is the one we are designing.</p>
</section>

<section id="together" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Combine them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A transponder and a Go.</strong> Air traffic control sees the transponder, and the light aircraft around you see the Go. Set the Go to the aircraft&rsquo;s ICAO address so both signals name one aircraft.</li>
    <li><strong>A FLARM and a Go.</strong> If your FLARM has no ADS-L, the Go adds it. A Go does not transmit FLARM.</li>
    <li><strong>SafeSky and a Go.</strong> The network brings the traffic far away, the radio the traffic close by, even with no coverage. <%= link_to_page "blog/network-or-radio", label: "Network or radio" %>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

